  "query_preview": "(This evening through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 303 PM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging over the Plains is flattening and moving \neastward as a potent shortwave digs southward into the Dakotas. This \nhas pushed the ridge axis over Indiana allowing temperatures to once \nagain warm up. Readings in the upper 80s to low 90s are expected \ntoday and Wednesday. The shortwave's attendant cold front arrives \nlate Wednesday, slowing in its forward speed and likely stalling \nover southern Indiana. Upper-level flow becomes parallel to the \nboundary allowing for repeated rounds of precipitation Wednesday \nevening into Thursday. The vort max departs later on Thursday with \nbroad ridging taking hold again over the Plains as we head into the \nweekend.\n\nWEDNESDAY\n\nSouthwesterly flow increases ahead of the approaching front late \nWednesday morning into the afternoon. This will promote strong low-\nlevel moisture advection with dew points rising into the 70s. \nCombined modest cold air advection aloft / height falls, strong \ninstability is expected by late Wednesday afternoon. Guidance shows \nbetween 2000-3000 J/Kg MLCAPE across southern Indiana decreasing \nwith northward extent. Shear increases as well due to the \napproaching shortwave, easterly at around 30kt. The front itself \nwill provide the forcing necessary for convection.\n\nGiven a shear vector largely parallel to the front, a linear \nconvective mode appears likely. Marginal to poor lapse rates should \nlimit severe potential. Mid-level dryness could lead to a downburst \nor two, given about 1000 J/Kg DCAPE. Our primary hazard appears to \nbe flash flooding, with storms potentially training due to the slow-\nmoving front and boundary-parellel flow. Convection may continue \ninto the night as additional rounds develop due to embedded \ndisturbances aloft. The best chance for both severe and hydro \nthreats is over our southern CWA where the front is most likely to \nstall.\n\nA potential failure mode for convection on Wednesday is related to \nconvection upstream tonight. Should an outflow boundary surge \nsouthward out of Illinois tomorrow morning, a low-level stable layer \nand cap may remain in place most of the day. This, as shown by the \n12Z HRRR may substantially limit convective development.\n\nTHURSDAY\n\nGuidance is in good agreement showing showers and thunderstorms \npersisting into the day Thursday, with a few differences. These \ndifferences are mainly regarding the northern extent of \nprecipitation chances. Global models such as the ECMWF and GFS tend \nto bring a slug of heavier precip right through central Indiana, \nwhere finer-scale models like the RAP and NAM are further south. For \nnow, we will nudge the forecast towards the southern side of \nguidance as there has been an overall trend in that direction today. \nThis all depends on where the front ends up stalling, however, which \nmay be influenced by Wednesday's convective evolution. Needless to \nsay, there is a bit of uncertainty in terms of precipitation \nplacement on Thursday.\n\nFRIDAY ONWARD\n\nCooler weather arrives Thursday and continues into Friday before \nridging returns on Saturday. Highs near 80 are expected to end the \nweek with temperatures rising back towards 90 over the weekend. \nTemps in the mid 80s to near 90 then persist into early next week. A \nweak front may arrive from the north on Sunday but model consensus \nis poor. PoPs will be limited to chance (40 percent) Sunday and \nMonday. Most locations should remain dry through the weekend.",
